  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 PM EST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nCurrent-Tonight... Surface observations and satellite imagery have \nshown weak cyclonic flow across central Florida as low pressure has \ndeveloped inland this afternoon. Bands of heavier rainfall wrap the \nnortheast side the developing low, now mostly remaining offshore and \nnorth of the local forecast area. Despite drying radar trends, \nFlood Warnings will likely remain in effect for portions of \nnorthern Brevard and eastern Volusia into the evening as flood \nwaters continue to slowly recede. A tight pressure gradient on the\nnortheast side of the low has brought windy and gusty conditions \nacross portions of Volusia county early today with peak gusts of \n35-40 mph, and a Wind Advisory remains in effect for the Volusia \ncoast through 7 PM. Low temperatures fall into the mid 60s. Will \nmonitor for patchy fog across southern portions of the area again \ntonight, but confidence remains too low to introduce to the \nforecast at this time. \n\nSaturday... The weak surface low continues to lift northward and \naway from the local area on Saturday. A surge of moisture builds \nsouth of the departing low and will support scattered showers (40-\n60%) across east central Florida. Southwest steering flow around 15-\n20 mph should limit prolonged periods of accumulating rainfall. \nHowever, moderate showers will have the potential to aggravate areas \nthat are already sensitive to flood impacts from this week's \nrainfall event, and a Marginal Risk of Excessive Rainfall exists \nacross areas from Brevard northward. Temperatures are forecast a \nfew degrees above seasonal values, ranging the upper 70s (north) \nand low 80s (south).\n\nA High Surf Advisory remains in effect through Saturday. Rough, \npounding surf will promote moderate to locally major beach erosion \nalong the coast. In addition, a High rip current threat will \ncontinue. Entering the rough and dangerous surf is strongly \ndiscouraged!\n\nSunday-Thursday (modified previous)...Aloft, troughiness moves \naway leaving zonal flow in its wake into Mon, then additional \nshortwave energy will traverse the FL peninsula into mid-week \nahead of a vigorous upper low digging across the Deep South/north \nGulf. At the surface, former low pressure off of the Carolina \ncoast will move up the Eastern Seaboard with drier air filtering \ndown the peninsula behind it. A weak surface boundary will press \nacross the central FL peninsula on Mon becoming quasi-stationary, \nthough we keep PoPs absent from the forecast mostly until Tue/Wed \n(20-30%) as moisture slowly increases back across the area. Max \ntemperatures will mostly range from the M70s to L80s (south) into \nWed, then U60s to L70s across the I-4 corridor on Thu, with M70s \nnear Lake Okee. Mins generally in the L-M60s thru Wed morning, \nexcept U50s possible for areas north/west of I-4. On Thu morning \nM50s possible N/W of I-4.",
